# SSH signing keys whose signatures make a cejel calibration record an attestation rather than
# a note. One line per signer: <principal> <keytype> <base64-key>, the format
# `git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` reads.
#
# This file names no key on purpose. Admitting one is an authority decision and belongs to the
# operator, not to an agent: the key added here is what this repository will thereafter accept as
# the operator's hand. `scripts/check-calibration-signatures.mjs` fails closed while the file is
# empty, so the guard refuses its own pull request until a key is added.
#
# To add yours, print the public key that signs your commits and append it, then commit that line
# yourself, signed:
#
#   git config --get user.signingkey
#   printf '%s %s\n' "<your-email>" "$(cat <path-to-key>.pub)" >> docs/security/allowed-signers
#
# An agent must not write a key into this file.
#
# What this file does not do: reach backwards. Every commit that touched docs/calibration/ before
# this gate reported %G? of N or E, and none reported G. They are listed in docs/security/README.md
# and stay unverifiable. This file governs what comes after it.
